## Answer

**Yes.** For every $n\times n$ tridiagonal symmetric positive-semidefinite matrix $A$ with nonnegative entries, there exists $D=\operatorname{diag}(d_1,\dots,d_n)$ with all $d_j>0$ such that $D^{T}AD$ is (weakly) diagonally dominant.

(Weak diagonal dominance is the only meaningful interpretation here: for $A=\begin{pmatrix}1&1\\1&1\end{pmatrix}$, which is PSD, the two row conditions force $d_1=d_2$ and then equality holds, so *strict* diagonal dominance is impossible in general.)

---

### Proof

Write
$$A=\begin{pmatrix} a_1 & b_1 & & \\ b_1 & a_2 & b_2 & \\ & \ddots & \ddots & \ddots \\ & & b_{n-1}& a_n\end{pmatrix},\qquad a_i\ge 0,\; b_i\ge 0.$$
For $D=\operatorname{diag}(x_1,\dots,x_n)$ with $x_i>0$, the matrix $D^{T}AD$ has diagonal entries $a_i x_i^2$ and off-diagonal entries $b_i x_i x_{i+1}$. Since all entries are nonnegative, weak row diagonal dominance is equivalent (after dividing each row by $x_i>0$) to
$$a_i x_i \;\ge\; b_{i-1}x_{i-1}+b_i x_{i+1},\qquad i=1,\dots,n,$$
with the convention $b_0=b_n=0$. So it suffices to find $x>0$ satisfying these inequalities.

**Step 1 — Reduction to the irreducible case.** If $b_k=0$ for some $k$, then $A$ splits into two independent tridiagonal blocks (indices $1,\dots,k$ and $k+1,\dots,n$), and we may choose $x$ independently on each block. A $1\times 1$ block $[a_i]$ with $a_i\ge 0$ is trivially diagonally dominant. Hence, by induction on the number of zero off-diagonals, it suffices to handle the **irreducible** case where **every $b_i>0$** (and $n\ge 2$).

**Step 2 — The pivot sequence.** Define
$$t_1:=a_1,\qquad t_{i+1}:=a_{i+1}-\frac{b_i^2}{t_i},\quad i=1,\dots,n-1,$$
i.e. the $t_i$ are the successive Schur complements (the diagonal pivots in the $LDL^{T}$ factorization of $A$). We claim:
$$t_1,t_2,\dots,t_{n-1}>0,\qquad t_n\ge 0.$$

We prove this by induction. Assume $t_1,\dots,t_{i-1}>0$ (vacuous for $i=1$). Then the leading $(i-1)\times(i-1)$ principal submatrix of $A$ is positive definite (its pivots are all positive), so the Schur complement $S^{(i)}$ obtained by eliminating the first $i-1$ rows/columns is well defined and positive semidefinite. $S^{(i)}$ is the $(n-i+1)\times(n-i+1)$ tridiagonal matrix
$$S^{(i)}=\begin{pmatrix} t_i & b_i & & \\ b_i & a_{i+1}& b_{i+1}& \\ &\ddots&\ddots&\ddots\end{pmatrix}.$$
Since $S^{(i)}\succeq 0$, its leading $2\times2$ principal submatrix $\begin{pmatrix}t_i&b_i\\ b_i&a_{i+1}\end{pmatrix}$ is PSD, giving
$$b_i^2\le t_i\,a_{i+1}.\tag{$\ast$}$$
Because $b_i>0$, ($\ast$) forces $t_i>0$ (if $t_i=0$ then $b_i^2\le 0$). This advances the induction for $i\le n-1$. Finally, ($\ast$) with $i=n-1$ gives $b_{n-1}^2\le t_{n-1}a_n$, i.e. $t_n=a_n-b_{n-1}^2/t_{n-1}\ge 0$. This proves the claim.

**Step 3 — Construction of $x$.** Set
$$r_i:=\frac{t_i}{b_i}>0\quad (i=1,\dots,n-1),\qquad x_1:=1,\qquad x_{i+1}:=r_i\,x_i.$$
All $x_i>0$. From $t_{i+1}=a_{i+1}-b_i^2/t_i$ we get $a_{i+1}=t_{i+1}+b_i/r_i$, i.e. for every $i$,
$$a_i = t_i + \frac{b_{i-1}}{r_{i-1}}\qquad (r_0:=\infty,\; b_0:=0).$$
Now verify each row:
- **Row 1:** $a_1 x_1 = t_1 x_1 = b_1 r_1 x_1 = b_1 x_2$. ✓
- **Row $i$, $2\le i\le n-1$:**
$$a_i x_i = \Bigl(t_i+\frac{b_{i-1}}{r_{i-1}}\Bigr)x_i = b_i r_i x_i + b_{i-1}x_{i-1}=b_i x_{i+1}+b_{i-1}x_{i-1}.$$ ✓
- **Row $n$:**
$$a_n x_n=\Bigl(t_n+\frac{b_{n-1}}{r_{n-1}}\Bigr)x_n = t_n x_n + b_{n-1}x_{n-1}\ge b_{n-1}x_{n-1},$$
since $t_n\ge 0$. ✓

Thus every row of $D^{T}AD$ is weakly diagonally dominant (in fact rows $1,\dots,n-1$ hold with equality, and row $n$ holds with slack $t_n\ge 0$).

**Step 4 — Assembly.** For the original (possibly reducible) matrix, apply the construction of Step 3 to each irreducible block independently and concatenate the resulting positive vectors. The zero off-diagonal entries between blocks contribute nothing to either side of the dominance inequalities, so the concatenated $x>0$ makes $D^{T}AD$ diagonally dominant. $\blacksquare$

